<section xml:id="sec-mod1-worked-examples">
    <title>Worked Examples</title>
    <p>Estimated time: 40 minutes.</p>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->

        <p>
          This section contains five carefully annotated worked examples.
          Each proof is written in the style expected in MAT 5610: complete
          mathematical prose, clearly organized, with every step justified.
          Read each proof twice: first for the mathematics, then for the
          <em>structure</em>. The annotations in brackets explain why each
          step is taken.
        </p>
        <p>
          <alert>How to use these examples.</alert> After reading each proof,
          cover the solution and try to reconstruct it from memory. Then
          compare your reconstruction to the original. Pay particular
          attention to the opening sentence of each proof, which announces
          the strategy.
        </p>
      <example xml:id="ex-mod1-we-1">
        <title>Quantifier Translation and Negation</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <alert>Problem.</alert> Let the universe be <m>\R</m>.
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li>
                Translate into symbols:
                <q>For every positive real number <m>\varepsilon</m>, there
                exists a natural number <m>N</m> such that <m>1/N \lt
                \varepsilon</m>.</q>
              </li>
              <li>Write the negation of your symbolic statement.</li>
              <li>Translate the negation back into plain English.</li>
            </ol>
          </p>
          <p>
            Before reading the solution, write your own symbolic translation
            and negation.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            <alert>Part (a): Symbolic translation.</alert>
            <em>[Identify each piece before writing the formula.
            The quantifiers appear in order: first
            <m>\varepsilon</m> is universally quantified, then <m>N</m> is
            existentially quantified.]</em>
          </p>
          <p>
            <md>
              \forall \varepsilon \gt 0 \;\; \exists N \in \N
              \text{ such that } \frac{1}{N} \lt \varepsilon.
            </md>
          </p>
          <p>
            <em>[Note: <m>\forall \varepsilon \gt 0</m> is shorthand for
            <m>\forall \varepsilon \in \R</m> with <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>.
            This is standard in analysis. You could also write
            <m>\forall \varepsilon \in (0, \infty)</m>.]</em>
          </p>
          <p>
            <alert>Part (b): Negation.</alert>
            <em>[To negate, flip every quantifier and negate the innermost
            predicate. The rule is: <m>\neg \forall = \exists</m> and
            <m>\neg \exists = \forall</m>. We also flip the inequality.]</em>
          </p>
          <p>
            <md>
              \exists \varepsilon \gt 0 \text{ such that }
              \forall N \in \N, \;\; \frac{1}{N} \geq \varepsilon.
            </md>
          </p>
          <p>
            <em>[Check: every <m>\forall</m> became <m>\exists</m>, every
            <m>\exists</m> became <m>\forall</m>, and <m>\lt</m> became
            <m>\geq</m>. The structure is completely parallel but reversed.]</em>
          </p>
          <p>
            <alert>Part (c): English translation of the negation.</alert>
            <q>There exists a positive real number <m>\varepsilon</m> such
            that <m>1/N \geq \varepsilon</m> for every natural number
            <m>N</m>.</q>
          </p>
          <p>
            <em>[Interpretation: the negation says that the numbers
            <m>1/N</m> have a positive lower bound: there is a single
            <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m> lying at or below every one of them.
            This is false, since the original statement is true
            (Archimedean Property; see
            <xref ref="par-mod1-sg-archimedean" text="custom">the three forms of the Archimedean Property</xref>
            in the Study Guide). But the negation is a valid, well-formed
            mathematical claim: it just happens to be false.]</em>
          </p>
          <p>
            <alert>Caution.</alert> The original statement is not the definition of
            <m>1/n \to 0</m>. That definition
            (<xref ref="par-mod1-sg-syn" text="custom">the convergence definition</xref>
            in the Study Guide) has a third quantifier: for every
            <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m> there is an <m>N</m> such that
            <m>1/n \lt \varepsilon</m> for <em>every</em>
            <m>n \geq N</m>. The statement in this example asks only for
            one term <m>1/N</m> below <m>\varepsilon</m>. For this
            particular sequence the two agree, because the terms decrease:
            if <m>1/N \lt \varepsilon</m> and <m>n \geq N</m>, then
            <m>1/n \leq 1/N \lt \varepsilon</m>. For a sequence that is not
            monotone, one term below <m>\varepsilon</m> proves nothing about
            the later terms.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </example>

      <example xml:id="ex-mod1-we-2">
        <title>Proof by Contradiction</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <alert>Claim.</alert> If <m>a</m> is rational and <m>b</m> is
            irrational, then <m>a + b</m> is irrational.
          </p>
          <p>
            Before reading the solution, attempt a proof of the claim.
            Which strategy will you use?
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            <em>[Strategy choice: We want to show <m>a + b</m> is irrational.
            Direct proof seems hard here: irrationality means
            <q>not rational,</q> which is negative information. Proof by
            contradiction is a natural fit.]</em>
          </p>
          <proof>
            <p>
              Assume, toward contradiction, that <m>a + b</m> is rational.
            </p>
            <p>
              <em>[Contradiction setup: we assume the opposite of what we want
              to prove. We now have two assumptions: <m>a</m> is rational,
              and <m>a + b</m> is rational. We expect to derive a contradiction
              of the assumption that <m>b</m> is irrational.]</em>
            </p>
            <p>
              Since <m>a</m> is rational and <m>a + b</m> is rational
              (by assumption), and since the rationals are closed under
              subtraction, the quantity
              <md>(a + b) - a = b</md>
              is rational.
            </p>
            <p>
              <em>[Key step: we used closure of <m>\Q</m> under
              subtraction. This is analogous to closure under addition:
              both require writing out the definitions and checking
              denominators. Here we cite it as a known fact.]</em>
            </p>
            <p>
              But this contradicts the hypothesis that <m>b</m> is irrational.
              Therefore, our assumption was false, and <m>a + b</m> must be
              irrational.
            </p>
            <p>
              <em>[The contradiction closes the proof. Notice the final
              sentence explicitly states what was shown and how.]</em>
            </p>
          </proof>
        </solution>
      </example>

      <example xml:id="ex-mod1-we-3">
        <title>Proof by Contrapositive</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <alert>Claim.</alert> For integers <m>m</m> and <m>n</m>, if
            <m>mn</m> is odd, then both <m>m</m> and <m>n</m> are odd.
          </p>
          <p>
            Before reading the solution, identify the contrapositive of
            the claim. Which logical law do you need to form the negation
            of <q><m>m</m> is odd and <m>n</m> is odd</q>?
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            <em>[Identify the structure: <m>P</m> = <q><m>mn</m> is odd</q>;
            <m>Q</m> = <q><m>m</m> is odd and <m>n</m> is odd</q>. The
            contrapositive is: if <m>m</m> is even or <m>n</m> is even, then
            <m>mn</m> is even. Note: <m>\neg(A \wedge B) \equiv \neg A \vee \neg B</m>
            by De Morgan's law.]</em>
          </p>
          <proof>
            <p>
              We prove the contrapositive: if <m>m</m> or <m>n</m> is even,
              then <m>mn</m> is even.
            </p>
            <p>
              <em>[The opening sentence announces the strategy and states the
              contrapositive clearly. This is good mathematical prose: the
              reader knows immediately what to expect.]</em>
            </p>
            <p>
              Without loss of generality, suppose <m>m</m> is even.
            </p>
            <p>
              <em>[<q>Without loss of generality</q> (WLOG) is justified here
              because the argument for <m>n</m> even is identical by symmetry.
              Only use WLOG when the cases truly are symmetric.]</em>
            </p>
            <p>
              Then <m>m = 2k</m> for some integer <m>k</m>. Therefore,
              <md>mn = (2k)n = 2(kn),</md>
              which is even, since <m>kn</m> is an integer.
            </p>
            <p>
              <em>[The computation is brief but complete. We use the definition
              of <q>even</q> (divisible by 2) in both the hypothesis and the
              conclusion.]</em>
            </p>
            <p>
              By the contrapositive, if <m>mn</m> is odd, then both <m>m</m>
              and <m>n</m> are odd.
            </p>
            <p>
              <em>[The final sentence explicitly invokes the contrapositive to
              close the loop. Do not leave the reader to infer this step.]</em>
            </p>
          </proof>
        </solution>
      </example>


    <!-- ============================================================ -->

      <example xml:id="ex-mod1-we-4">
        <title>Proof by Mathematical Induction</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <alert>Claim.</alert> For all integers <m>n \geq 1</m>,
            <md>\sum_{k=1}^{n} (2k - 1) = n^2.</md>
            (The left side is the sum of the first <m>n</m> odd numbers.)
          </p>
          <p>
            Before reading the solution, verify the formula for
            <m>n = 1, 2, 3</m>. Then attempt the induction proof,
            being sure to label the base case, induction hypothesis,
            and inductive step.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            <em>[Verify a few cases first: <m>n = 1</m>: left-hand side (LHS) = 1, right-hand side (RHS) = 1.
            <m>n = 2</m>: LHS = <m>1 + 3 = 4</m>, RHS = 4.
            <m>n = 3</m>: LHS = <m>1 + 3 + 5 = 9</m>, RHS = 9.
            Good. Now write the formal proof.]</em>
          </p>
          <proof>
            <p>
              We prove the claim by mathematical induction. Let <m>P(n)</m>
              denote the statement
              <m>\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^n (2k-1) = n^2</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
              <em>[Name your predicate. This makes the base case and inductive
              step easier to state precisely.]</em>
            </p>
            <p>
              <alert>Base case</alert> <m>(n = 1)</m>: The left side is
              <m>\sum_{k=1}^1 (2k-1) = 2(1) - 1 = 1</m>. The right side
              is <m>1^2 = 1</m>. Since they are equal, <m>P(1)</m> holds.
            </p>
            <p>
              <em>[The base case is verified explicitly, not just asserted.
              For simple cases one line suffices.]</em>
            </p>
            <p>
              <alert>Inductive step.</alert> Assume <m>P(n)</m> holds for
              some integer <m>n \geq 1</m>; that is, assume
              <md>\sum_{k=1}^{n} (2k-1) = n^2. \quad\text{(Induction Hypothesis)}</md>
              We must show <m>P(n+1)</m>: that <m>\sum_{k=1}^{n+1}(2k-1) = (n+1)^2</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
              <em>[State the induction hypothesis explicitly and label it.
              Then state what you must prove. This structure is non-negotiable
              in a correct induction proof.]</em>
            </p>
            <p>
              Computing:
              <md>
                <mrow>\sum_{k=1}^{n+1}(2k-1)
                \;&amp;=\; \left(\sum_{k=1}^{n}(2k-1)\right) + (2(n+1) - 1)</mrow>
                <mrow>\;&amp;=\; n^2 + (2n + 1)
                \quad\text{(Induction Hypothesis)}</mrow>
                <mrow>\;&amp;=\; (n+1)^2.</mrow>
              </md>
            </p>
            <p>
              <em>[The second line cites the induction hypothesis. Always
              label where you use it. This is the heart of the argument.]</em>
            </p>
            <p>
              This is exactly <m>P(n+1)</m>, so the inductive step is complete.
            </p>
            <p>
              <alert>Conclusion.</alert> By the principle of mathematical
              induction, <m>P(n)</m> holds for all integers <m>n \geq 1</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
              <em>[The conclusion explicitly invokes the induction principle.
              Do not end an induction proof without this closing statement.]</em>
            </p>
          </proof>
        </solution>
      </example>

      <example xml:id="ex-mod1-we-5">
        <title>An Iff Proof (Two Directions)</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <alert>Claim.</alert> An integer <m>n</m> is even if and only if
            <m>n^2</m> is even.
          </p>
          <p>
            An iff proof requires two separate arguments. Before reading,
            identify which strategy you expect to use for each direction,
            and why the two directions may require different approaches.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            <em>[An <q>iff</q> proof requires proving both directions:
            <m>n \text{ even} \Rightarrow n^2 \text{ even}</m> and
            <m>n^2 \text{ even} \Rightarrow n \text{ even}</m>.
            These may require different strategies.]</em>
          </p>
          <proof>
            <p>
              <m>(\Rightarrow)</m> <em>Assume <m>n</m> is even. We show
              <m>n^2</m> is even.</em>
            </p>
            <p>
              <em>[This direction is direct: we have concrete information
              about <m>n</m>.]</em>
            </p>
            <p>
              Since <m>n</m> is even, write <m>n = 2m</m> for some integer
              <m>m</m>. Then <m>n^2 = (2m)^2 = 4m^2 = 2(2m^2)</m>, which
              is even.
            </p>
            <p>
              <m>(\Leftarrow)</m> <em>Assume <m>n^2</m> is even. We show
              <m>n</m> is even.</em>
            </p>
            <p>
              <em>[This direction has an awkward hypothesis. We know
              <m>n^2 = 2j</m> for some integer <m>j</m>, but it is not
              obvious how to get from there to a statement about <m>n</m>.
              The contrapositive is cleaner: if <m>n</m> is odd, then
              <m>n^2</m> is odd.]</em>
            </p>
            <p>
              We prove the contrapositive: if <m>n</m> is odd, then
              <m>n^2</m> is odd.
              Suppose <m>n</m> is odd, so <m>n = 2m + 1</m> for some
              integer <m>m</m>. Then
              <md>n^2 = (2m+1)^2 = 4m^2 + 4m + 1 = 2(2m^2 + 2m) + 1,</md>
              which is odd.
            </p>
            <p>
              <em>[The two directions used different strategies: direct for
              <m>(\Rightarrow)</m> and contrapositive for
              <m>(\Leftarrow)</m>. This is entirely normal and often
              necessary. Do not force both directions into the same
              strategy.]</em>
            </p>
            <p>
              Since both directions hold, <m>n</m> is even if and only if
              <m>n^2</m> is even.
            </p>
          </proof>
        </solution>
      </example>


    <!-- ============================================================ -->

      <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod1-we-reflection">
        <title>Reflection Questions</title>
        <p>
          After reading through the worked examples, consider the following.
          These questions ask you to engage with the examples analytically,
          not just to read them passively.
        </p>
      </paragraphs>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-we-6">
        <title>WLOG Justified</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            In <xref ref="ex-mod1-we-3"/>, the proof uses <q>without loss of generality</q>
            to handle the case that <m>n</m> is even. Write out the
            full argument for that case explicitly, to confirm that WLOG
            was justified.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-we-7">
        <title>Mixed Strategies in Iff Proofs</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            In <xref ref="ex-mod1-we-5"/>, the proof uses two different proof strategies for
            the two directions of the iff. Identify another iff statement
            (from this module or from calculus) and determine which
            strategy each direction would likely require.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-we-8">
        <title>Opening Line of a Convergence Proof</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Look back at the
            <xref ref="par-mod1-sg-syn" text="custom">convergence definition</xref>
            at the end of the Study Guide. If you were writing a proof that
            <m>a_n \to L</m>, what would your opening line look like?
            (Hint: what must you produce to satisfy the definition?)
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>


  </section>
